\section{Rotating a product out of symplectic position}
\label{sec:product-rotation}

The capacity of a product of two planar convex bodies depends on how its
factor planes sit in symplectic space. If the planes are symplectically
orthogonal, the capacity is the smaller factor area. We show that this
arrangement never maximizes capacity among rigid rotations of the product:
a small rotation increases capacity while preserving volume.

Let $A,B\subset\mathbb R^2$ be convex bodies with nonempty interiors, with
areas $a\le b$. We keep the Euclidean body $A\times B$ fixed and vary the
symplectic structure. This is equivalent to rotating the body while keeping
the symplectic structure fixed. With
\[
 J_2=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\-1&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
 J_\theta=
 \begin{pmatrix}
  \cos\theta\,J_2&\sin\theta\,I_2\\
  -\sin\theta\,I_2&-\cos\theta\,J_2
 \end{pmatrix},
 \qquad 0\le\theta\le\frac\pi2,
\]
write $c_\theta$ for the Ekeland--Hofer--Zehnder capacity relative to
$J_\theta$. At $\theta=0$ the factor planes are symplectic, with opposite
orientations, and $c_0=a$. At $\theta=\pi/2$ both factor planes are Lagrangian;
write $\ell=c_{\pi/2}$. The relative coordinates of $A$ and $B$ are fixed
throughout this path. The parameter $\theta$ describes the symplectic form:
the corresponding rigid rotation has angle $\theta/2$, as verified below.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:product-initial-rotation}
For these factors,
\[
 c_\theta=\frac{a}{\cos\theta}
 \quad\text{whenever}\quad
 0\le\tan\theta\le\frac{\ell}{2a}.
\]
Consequently, every sufficiently small positive rotation along this path
increases both capacity and systolic ratio.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
First suppose that $A$ and $B$ are polygons containing the origin in their
interiors. For each facet write $u_i=n_i/h_i$ for its support-normalized
normal. Haim-Kislev's formula \cite[Theorem~1.1]{HK2017} expresses reciprocal
capacity as the maximum ordered symplectic pairing sum, with factor $2$,
over weights satisfying
\[
 \lambda_i\ge0,\qquad \sum_i\lambda_i=1,
 \qquad\sum_i\lambda_i u_i=0.
\]
For a product, the last equation holds separately in each factor. Let $t$
be the total weight on facets from $A$. Dividing those weights by $t$ gives
a feasible planar configuration, so their contribution to reciprocal
capacity is at most $t^2/a$. The corresponding bound for $B$ is
$(1-t)^2/b$. Reversing a planar order accommodates the opposite sign in
the second diagonal block of $J_0$.

For the mixed contribution, divide the $A$ weights by $2t$ and the $B$
weights by $2(1-t)$. The resulting weights have total mass one, satisfy
closure, and are feasible for the Lagrangian endpoint. Its reciprocal
capacity is $1/\ell$, so the original mixed contribution is at most
$4t(1-t)/\ell$. The cases $t=0,1$ have no mixed contribution. Linearity in
the symplectic form therefore gives
\begin{equation}\label{eq:product-mass-bound}
 \frac1{c_\theta}\le
 \max_{0\le t\le1}\left[
  \cos\theta\left(\frac{t^2}{a}+\frac{(1-t)^2}{b}\right)
  +\frac{4\sin\theta}{\ell}t(1-t)\right].
\end{equation}
Putting all weight in $A$ also gives $1/c_\theta\ge\cos\theta/a$.

Set $U=\cos\theta/a$, $V=\cos\theta/b$ and
$W=4\sin\theta/\ell$. Then $V\le U$, and the expression in brackets minus
$U$ is
\[
 (1-t)\bigl[V-U+t(W-U-V)\bigr].
\]
The inner bracket is affine in $t$; its endpoint values are $V-U$ and
$W-2U$. Both are nonpositive when
$\tan\theta\le\ell/(2a)$. The two reciprocal-capacity bounds then agree.
Polygonal approximation and continuity of capacity extend the bounds,
and hence the formula, to arbitrary planar convex bodies.

Finally, $\ell>0$, so the interval contains positive angles. For each such
angle, $a/\cos\theta>a$. The underlying rigid rotation preserves the
four-dimensional volume $ab$, hence also increases $c_\theta^2/(2ab)$.
\end{proof}

To see the rotation explicitly, put
\[
 K=\begin{pmatrix}0&J_2\\J_2&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
 R_\theta=\cos(\theta/2)I_4+\sin(\theta/2)K.
\]
Since $K^T=-K$ and $K^2=-I_4$, the matrix $R_\theta$ is orthogonal with
determinant one. Direct multiplication gives
$J_\theta=R_\theta J_0R_\theta^T$. Thus $c_\theta$ is the capacity of
$R_\theta^T(A\times B)$ with respect to $J_0$. This rotation is not
symplectic with respect to $J_0$ for positive $\theta$ in the displayed
interval; symplectic rotations would preserve capacity.

\begin{example}\label{ex:product-endpoints}
The Lagrangian arrangement can have smaller capacity than the symplectic
arrangement. Take
\[
 A=\operatorname{conv}\{(-1,-1),(2,-1),(-1,2)\},\qquad
 B=\begin{pmatrix}2&0\\0&1/2\end{pmatrix}A.
\]
Both areas are $9/2$. To show that the Lagrangian capacity is smaller,
it suffices to exhibit one feasible configuration in Haim-Kislev's formula.
The support-normalized normals are
\[
 u_0=(0,-1),\quad u_1=(1,1),\quad u_2=(-1,0),\qquad
 v_0=(0,-2),\quad v_1=(1/2,2),\quad v_2=(-1/2,0).
\]
Give all six normals weight $1/6$ and order them as
$(u_0,v_0,u_1,v_1,u_2,v_2)$. Both triples sum to zero, so the configuration
satisfies closure. Its signed cross-pairing sum is $5$, giving
\[
 \frac1\ell\ge 2\left(\frac16\right)^2\!5=\frac5{18},
 \qquad\text{and hence}\qquad
 \ell\le\frac{18}{5}<\frac92=c_0.
\]
\end{example}

\begin{remark}
If $\ell\le a$, Theorem~\ref{thm:product-initial-rotation} gives capacities
larger than both endpoint values at small positive angles. By continuity,
the maximum along this path is attained in its interior. The triangle
example satisfies this condition.
\end{remark}
